WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.520
- Coinbase CEO, Brian Armstrong,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.500
is in a battle with regulators.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.120
The SEC sued his company,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.910
saying Coinbase broke the
rules by listing tokens

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.420
that the regulator alleges are
subject to securities laws.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.510
He sat down with "The Journal"
to discuss the situation.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.280
This doesn't seem like
one where there's a lot

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.410
of dispute about the facts.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.040
You are trading
cryptocurrencies on Coinbase.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.950
The question really is,
are you breaking the law?

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.830
And is what you are trading a security?

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.510
What's your interpretation of the law?

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.830
- Basically, we're in this environment

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.110
of regulation by enforcement.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.790
I don't feel like there's
a clear rule book.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.740
The only sort of high level
statements they've made

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.660
is that everything other
than Bitcoin is a security,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.270
which that's just not
what it says in the law.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.650
And by the way, that
would also kind of mean

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.420
like the end of the crypto
industry in the U.S.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.400
if that was actually what happened.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.500
And so we have to go to
court to challenge that.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.330
We're proud to do it for the
industry and for America.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.170
- But this has been something

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.000
that has been hanging over the industry

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.530
and Coinbase for a while.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.930
You and your S1 when
you filed to go public,

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.520
put down as a risk.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.040
The SEC may decide that the
stuff that we are trading

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.420
is in fact a security.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.030
But you pushed ahead anyway
and built a business,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.460
and grew that business
and added more tokens

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.320
and added more assets.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.610
Tell us why you did that.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.010
- Well, there are certainly
crypto securities out there.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.380
In fact, Coinbase has reviewed

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.330
well over 1,000 different assets.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.950
90% of them we've rejected.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.200
The 10% or less of assets
that we've reviewed

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.220
that we did decide to list,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.710
we feel that those are crypto commodities.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.400
And so they don't need to be registered

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.140
and traded with under an SEC license.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.000
- So the question of what is a security,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:33.900
to zoom way out,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.330
you've got like baseball cards at one end,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.370
you've got Apple stock
on the other end, right?

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.170
Baseball cards, clearly not a security.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.370
We trade baseball cards.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.810
They have value, but nobody thinks

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.810
that like a 1987 tops Daryl
Strawberry is a security.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.950
Apple stock, clearly a security.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.110
Big gap between those things.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.430
And the SEC is making arguments

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.100
that a bunch of things
that you trade on Coinbase

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.740
are more over here, closer to Apple.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.110
How do you think about that distinction?

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.270
- There is this thing called
the Howey Test, right?

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.190
Which is the definition of a security.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.547
And it kind of says,

00:02:06.547 --> 00:02:10.020
"Is there an investment of
money in a common enterprise

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.460
with an expectation of profit

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.840
based on the effort of others?"

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.550
So it has those kind of four prongs.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:15.480
The important thing to know

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.720
is that all four of those
things have to be true, right?

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.040
So there's various ways

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.610
that you could imagine an a crypto asset

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.960
would not be a security, right?

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.850
If it's sufficiently decentralized,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.220
there's no common enterprise, right?

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.080
If there's some specific
utility around it,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.220
it's not specifically,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.390
it's not just for the purpose

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.340
of the value of going up, right?

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.920
And so what we need in the
United States at this point

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.450
is regulatory clarity.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.380
- So you looked at
thousands or a 1,000 tokens,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.110
you listed 250-ish of them.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.140
And the SEC has a problem
with 13, at least 13,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.970
but they highlight 13.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.070
But the issue is you
have to bat 1,000, right?

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.320
You can't have any securities.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.890
You've gotta have all 250 that you trade

00:02:58.890 --> 00:02:59.910
can't be securities,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.670
otherwise, you're running an
unlicensed securities exchange,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.980
and an unlicensed clearing
house, and an unlicensed broker.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.690
So it seems like the odds are
kind of not in your favor.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.520
- So that's not really how I think of it.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.800
Remember, the goal of this lawsuit

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.200
is to get clarity for the industry, right?

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.810
And we've actually been
formally petitioning the SEC

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.637
for clarity for the long time.

00:03:20.637 --> 00:03:22.438
- The SEC's goal is to stop you

00:03:22.438 --> 00:03:24.750
from breaking the law on their view.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.940
It's not, I don't think they
think that there's a lack

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:27.870
of clarity in the industry.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.210
I think they think that
you're breaking the law.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.830
- Well, that may be what they say,

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:35.100
but I can assure you there's
a huge lack of clarity.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.070
But we feel strongly, all of
the ones that we listed there

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.260
since we took a pretty
conservative approach,

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.610
they are commodities.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.320
- The SEC says in its complaint

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.580
that you rushed that process.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:45.690
How do you respond to that?

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.490
- I feel pretty comfortable
about the analysis.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.550
The irony of this is that we
were continuously asking them

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.940
during this period, "Can
you give us more clarity?"

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.261
Like that's how it's supposed to work.

00:03:56.261 --> 00:03:57.720
- You have taken, I
think it's fair to say,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.600
a reasonably confrontational
approach for the SEC.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.490
There's a Twitter thread in which you said

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.620
there's some sketchy things going on.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.093
Do you regret that approach?

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.580
- Well, I wouldn't say our approach

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.780
has been confrontational.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.100
It is with great...

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.110
We've never relished the opportunity

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.360
or the idea of like
ending up in litigation

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.193
with our regulator.

00:04:16.193 --> 00:04:17.040
In fact, quite the opposite.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.230
And by the way, I have
a great deal of respect

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.650
for the people at the SEC,
the staff, the commissioners.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.450
I think our relationship with the chair

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.250
has been quite difficult, honestly.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.410
But there's a lot of really
good people at the SEC,

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.580
and we've had no issue

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.370
working with every other
regulator out there,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.865
both in the U.S., but
also internationally.

00:04:34.865 --> 00:04:37.200
- Why has the relationship
with Chair Gensler

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.043
been difficult?

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.223
- I don't know.

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:41.790
I don't wanna speculate on his motives

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.230
or anything like that.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:48.060
But when he first came into the role,

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.530
I made an effort to kind of fly out there

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.300
and go meet with him.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.240
I don't know why the
schedules wouldn't align.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.320
We made several attempts.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.380
We eventually did get to
meet, but only virtually-

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.690
- Have you ever had a one-on-one
meeting in person with him?

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.430
- I've never been able to
meet with him in person,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:03.450
which is rare.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.180
- What is sort of Brian Armstrong's dream

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.010
regulatory structure for crypto?

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.200
- Well, so the first one is we
just need to get some clarity

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.710
about the market structure
and how the CFTC and the SEC

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.180
are both gonna regulate this industry.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.320
What are the boundaries?

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.030
I think we also need to just bring in

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:19.410
some basic consumer protection.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.030
It's actually not rocket science.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.320
This is just applying

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.560
some of these really
basic common sense ideas

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.393
to the industry.

00:05:26.393 --> 00:05:29.550
And then once we have
that legislation in place,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.260
I think we'll start to see
some of the entrepreneurs

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.097
who've left the U.S. come back and say,

00:05:33.097 --> 00:05:36.240
"Okay, I feel that we're not
just gonna be attacked randomly

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.850
or have incredibly high legal
bills at any given moment,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.140
and we can actually build a business

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.340
here in the U.S. again."

